WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.600
Tool calling lets an AI application request a bounded capability from application code or another service.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.860
An agent may plan steps and choose tools to invoke.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:22.320
Tools may read information, search documents, create tickets, send email, update calendars, query databases, call APIs, interact with code, browse approved resources, or operate workflows.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:28.380
The security risk increases when model output can influence tool selection, arguments, or repeated actions.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.360
Read-only tools and write-capable tools deserve different controls.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:46.180
A read-only status lookup can still expose sensitive data, but a write-capable tool can change systems, send communications, modify records, approve access, deploy code, create cost, or trigger external effects.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.960
Least privilege should apply to every tool credential.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:56.300
A tool should receive only the permissions, data scope, rate limit, and destination access needed for its approved purpose.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.960
Model output should not directly become a high-impact action.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:11.380
The application should validate tool arguments, enforce allowlisted actions, check user authorization, constrain resource identifiers, and require approval gates for sensitive operations.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.080
Dry-run behavior can help users inspect what would happen before the action occurs.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:27.740
Scoped credentials, per-user authorization checks, and durable audit trails make it easier to understand who requested an action, what the model proposed, and what the application actually executed.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.440
Agent behavior needs operational limits.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:38.900
Planning loops, retries, tool chains, and autonomous work should have budgets, stop conditions, rate limits, and monitoring.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:47.840
A secure agent should not be able to expand its own authority, invent new tools, bypass approvals, or use retrieved content as permission to act.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:57.420
The application remains responsible for controlling tool access, validating arguments, recording decisions, and escalating uncertain or high-impact cases to humans.
